  "explanation": "$E(X^2) = \\int_0^1 2x^3\\,dx = \\Big[\\frac{x^4}{2}\\Big]_0^1 = \\frac{1}{2}$. $\\operatorname{Var}(X) = \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{4}{9} = \\frac{1}{18} \\approx 0.0556$. Subtracting $\\frac{2}{3}$ without squaring gives a negative number, which a variance can never be.",
  "workedSolution": "1. $E(X^2) = \\int_0^1 2x^3\\,dx = \\Big[\\frac{x^4}{2}\\Big]_0^1 = \\frac{1}{2}$. $\\operatorname{Var}(X) = \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{4}{9} = \\frac{1}{18} \\approx 0.0556$. Subtracting $\\frac{2}{3}$ without squaring gives a negative number, which a variance can never be.",
  "hints": [
   "$E(X^2) = \\int x^2 f(x)\\,dx$."
